## Recommendations

{% if public_functions %}
1. **Lean on the public API.** The functions exposed at
   the module level are the stable interface —
   everything starting with an underscore is internal
   and can change without notice.

{{ m.related_functions(public_functions, limit=5) }}

2. **Read the docstrings first, then the source.** Every
   public function above has a one-line docstring that
   explains its intent. Start there, not with the body.

3. **Run the related tests before making changes.** The
   test file names tell you which test exercises which
   function — target with
   `pytest -k "{{ feature_name }}"`.
{% elif public_classes %}
1. **Prefer composition over subclassing.** The classes
   in {{ title | lower }} are designed to be used, not
   extended. If you find yourself needing a subclass,
   check whether the base class already exposes the
   extension point you want.

{{ m.related_classes(public_classes, limit=5) }}

2. **Respect constructor signatures.** The `__init__`
   methods document the required state for each class —
   pass all the arguments, even the ones that look
   optional.
{% else %}
1. **Start small.** Read one source file at a time and
   build a mental model from the bottom up.

2. **Write a throwaway test first.** Even a five-line
   test that exercises the happy path will teach you
   more than reading the source alone.
{% endif %}

## Why this matters

Following these tips avoids the most common
time-wasters when working with this part of the
codebase. They are not strict rules — use judgment —
but deviating from them usually signals that you are
fighting the design rather than using it.

